📑 Architectural Blueprint OverviewThe goal of this system is to transform clothing and gear selection in the Pro Shop into a tactical, defensive layer where athlete skill progression (fuel, nitro, and torque ratings) reacts dynamically to the environment of historical tracking runs.This document serves as the master engineering reference for when you choose to implement this mechanic in your upload_ui.py pipeline at a later stage.1. Data Schema AdaptationsTo make the engine work seamlessly for historical imports without overcomplicating your player save-file structure, categorize items based on their core environmental utility.Item Attribute Definitions (gear_catalog)Every item in the pro shop catalog receives a weather metadata property mapping to one of four states:🌤️ All-Weather (Baseline wildcard — never triggers penalties, acts as a safe buffer).💨 Windbreaker (Windy) (Aerodynamic drag reduction).🌧️ Rain Jacket (Wet) (Hydrophobic storm insulation).❄️ Winter Jacket (Cold) (Thermal heat retention).Weather Condition ThresholdsWhen an activity file is loaded, the engine reads or calculates the climate context:Cold Environment: Ambient temperature < 45.0°FHot Environment: Ambient temperature > 75.0°FWet Environment: Activity precipitation flagged as rain or track wetness.Windy Environment: Wind speeds exceeding a benchmark threshold (e.g., > 12 mph).2. The Execution PipelineWhen a batch of activities is committed via upload_ui.py, each run processes through a four-stage validation loop before updating your master player save profile:text[Incoming Activity] ➔ [1. Climate Profiler] ➔ [2. Kit Audit] ➔ [3. Penalty/Bonus Calculator] ➔ [4. State Commit]
Use code with caution.Stage 1: The Climate ProfilerFor each activity, the engine extracts environmental data:Checks for native telemetry data fields (like ambient_temp_f from Garmin .FIT rows).If missing (such as in basic .GPX or .CSV tracks), it parses the activity timestamp's month string as a chronological fallback (e.g., December/January default to Cold, July/August default to Hot).Stage 2: The Loadout Kit AuditThe engine checks what the player currently has active on their dashboard profile (player.equipped_jacket_name, player.equipped_pants_name, etc.) and compiles an active array of equipped weather traits.Stage 3: The Penalty/Bonus MatcherThe logic branches into three potential calculations based on the alignment of Stage 1 and Stage 2:Scenario A: The Exposure Penalty (Under-dressed):Condition: Climate is Cold, but the active kit lacks any item with the Winter Jacket (Cold) trait.Math: Deducts a fixed modifier (e.g., -15 Endurance Rating, -10 Velocity Rating) from the final baseline calculations of that run.Scenario B: The Overheating Penalty (Over-dressed):Condition: Climate is Hot, but the active kit contains an item with the Winter Jacket (Cold) trait.Math: Deducts a heavy performance drain (e.g., -20 Endurance Rating, -15 Velocity Rating) because the athlete is over-insulating in peak warmth.Scenario C: The Synchronization Multiplier (Perfect Prep):Condition: The active kit matches the specific weather tier perfectly, triggering a full Outfit Synced state.Math: Protects against all exposure decay and applies a global 1.20x scaling multiplier (+20% bonus) to all positive rating point gains scored during that tracking session.Stage 4: Feedback CompilationThe engine appends a clear string payload property (weather_feedback) directly into the activity dictionary object, ensuring the user interface can display exactly why a rating fluctuated when looking at their session history logs.3. Gameplay Safety Guards & Balancing RulesTo keep the game fun and prevent players from falling into progress "death spirals" during long historical uploads, the implementation relies on three structural safety constraints:The Indoor/Treadmill Safety Override:Add a simple toggle widget or tracking flag check (is_indoor = True). If triggered, the script completely bypasses the environmental checks, setting the profile to a baseline 72°F environment. This prevents treadmill runs from getting penalized for freezing outside winter conditions.The Batch Penalty Cap:Instead of allowing sequential runs to continuously subtract progress until a player drops to Level 1, enforce a global batch floor. A single bulk file commit can never reduce an athlete's macro skill ratings by more than a set threshold (e.g., a maximum loss cap of 45 points per button click).Wildcard Cushioning:Items marked as All-Weather (your default sneakers, standard tracking watches, and basic shorts) are completely immune to temperature friction. They never trigger overheating penalties in the summer, nor do they trigger freeze exposure in the winter, keeping the focus entirely on specialized gear (Jackets, Pants, and Head Gear).
